  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMorning fog and stratus from Saturday's rain will slowly lift and \nmix out by early afternoon and allow for, at minimum, filtered \nsunshine and pockets of heating ahead of the approach of today's \ncold front. Both thermodynamics and kinematics will be favorable for \norganized strong to severe thunderstorms along the front today. HREF \nand REFS mean SBCAPE is >1500 J/kg, and as a mid-level trough \nencroaches with increasing westerly 45-55 knot flow overspreading \nnorth to south, effective shear values ramp up to 40-50 knots with \nhighest values in NW PA (40-60% probability of >1500 J/kg of SBCAPE \nand >40 knots of deep layer shear). Plenty of surface moisture is in \nplace this morning with upper 60s to low 70s dew points, though if \nclouds do end up scattering out by early afternoon, there will \nlikely be some mixing of dew points. But, the typically over-mixed \nHRRR still maintains upper 60s by mid-afternoon. All that said, the \nprimary inhibiting feature over the past 24-36 hours has been a \nsubsident warm layer around 800 mb and perhaps another weaker one \nabove 500 mb. One noted difference in the Sunday morning 12z CAMs is \nthat pre-frontal surface temperatures are forecast to rise a bit \nhigher than previous runs had suggested. The cap(s) are still there, \nbut with warmer surface temps, the ML parcel trace could skirt by \nthe caps, or at least have an easier time eroding them with stronger \nforcing along the front. It'll likely take the stronger forcing to \nreally get things going today, so wouldn't expect too much pre-\nfrontal stuff, though a lake breeze could spark off some priming \nconvection, but most development should occur within the strongest \nconvergence right along the boundary. Environmental mid-level lapse \nrates aren't all that great either, but there is some room to wet-\nbulb, so it's certainly plausible that some storms do do this and \ncan improve them on the mesoscale. SPC has introduced an upgrade to \na Slight Risk (2/5) for the northern half of our area (Pittsburgh \nnorth).\n\nTiming remains relatively consistent among most models with \ninitiation around 4-5pm, perhaps an hour earlier accounting for CAM \nbiases, in NW PA and then working southwest. That's where the best \nsevere threat will be, and how far southwest it persists will be \ndependent on progression of convection as we'll be running out of \ndaylight given sunset moving earlier and earlier by mid September. \nPrimary threats will be large hail and damaging wind with a low \ntornado threat as hodographs look straight and more supportive of \nsplitting storms. They also look to be progressive, so hydro \nconcerns should be more limited today only to areas that were \nespecially hit hard on Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsembles have clustered into better agreement that the trough axis \ncrosses the region early Monday and maintains scattered shower \novernight that exit our eastern ridges by the morning hours. Mid-\nlevel ridging builds and provides a dry interlude through at least \nmost of Tuesday before another trough approaches, crosses to our \nnorth, and returns showers Tuesday evening (faster solution) or \nWednesday morning (more likely slower solution). Machine learning \nsuggests a low-end severe and flooding threat both Wednesday and \nThursday as dew points start to recover a bit back into the 60s on \nthe south side of an east-west baroclinic zone.",
